        "title": "10. Effects and external systems",
        "takeaway": "Use Effects to synchronize external systems, not to calculate ordinary derived values.",
        "sections": [
          {
            "title": "Build the mental model",
            "paragraphs": [
              "An Effect can connect a component to something outside rendering, such as browser storage or a subscription. Cleanup releases resources or prevents obsolete work from affecting the current screen.",
              "Fetching data in an Effect needs loading and error handling plus protection from stale results. Frameworks and data libraries can manage these concerns. Do not suppress dependencies just to stop an Effect from running."
            ],
            "example": "useEffect(() => {\n  const controller = new AbortController();\n  fetch(\"/api/lessons\", {signal: controller.signal})\n    .then(r => { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(\"HTTP \" + r.status); return r.json(); })\n    .then(data => { /* validate shape, then update data state */ })\n    .catch(error => { if (error.name !== \"AbortError\") { /* show error state */ } });\n  return () => controller.abort();\n}, []);\n// Pattern fragment: import useEffect and supply a real API; comments are required implementation work."
          },
          {
            "title": "Common mistake to avoid",
            "paragraphs": [
              "An Effect may be set up and cleaned up extra times in development Strict Mode. Design correct cleanup instead of relying on a single execution."
            ]
          }
        ],
